<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonzalo Guerrero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1536</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonzalo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> was a Spanish sailor from Palos who was shipwrecked on the coast of Yucatán in 1511 and enslaved by the Maya. He later rose in the service of the lord of Chetumal, married into a noble family, adopted Maya dress and tattoos, and refused to rejoin the Spanish when Cortés arrived in 1519. He is said to have advised the Maya against the conquistadors and died fighting the Spanish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> around 1536.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonzalo-guerrero.jpg" alt="Gonzalo Guerrero" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gonzalo_Guerrero.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>'s life before 1511, when the caravel Santa María de la Barca, carrying him and 20 to 30 passengers, was wrecked in a tropical storm between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. The survivors drifted in rowing skiffs for thirteen days before sighting the eastern coast of Yucatán, by which time only ten were still alive. On landing they were seized by the Maya militia of Waymil; some were ritually sacrificed and the rest were forced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> under provincial aristocrats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In about 1514 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> entered the service of Nachan Can, the halach uinich or governor of Chetumal, while his crew-mate Gerónimo de Aguilar remained a slave of the batab of Xamanha. Guerrero is thought to have earned the rank of nakom, or commanding officer, and to have served Chetumal or its allies against the expeditions of Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1517 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a> in 1518. He converted to Maya polytheism and married a Maya woman believed to be the governor's daughter, and their children are counted among the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed on Cozumel on 6 March 1519 he invited both Spaniards to join him. According to the conquistador Bernal Díaz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> told Aguilar that he had three children, that the Maya looked on him as a lord and a captain in war, and that his face was tattooed and his ears pierced. His wife, Zazil Há, angrily told Aguilar to leave them alone.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> went on to lead the Maya against the Spanish. He is thought to have been central to the failure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a>'s entrada of 1527–1528: Chetumal sent his awaited reinforcements off course with false scouts, and Montejo sailed away in 1528 without fighting. Guerrero is also thought to have fought Alonso de Avila's entrada of 1531–1533, which took the provincial capital but in the end failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Late in 1535 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> is thought to have led fifty canoes across the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> to support the cacique Cicumba against the Spanish villa of Buena Esperanza in the Sula Valley. In 1536 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> stormed Cicumba's fortified camp on the Ulua River. Andrés de Cereceda, writing to the king on 14 August 1536, reported that the tattooed body of a Spaniard killed by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a> shot was found after the battle; he named the man Gonzalo Aroca, but historians identify him as Guerrero.</p><p>No writing by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> is known to survive. Reviled by the sixteenth-century chroniclers, he was later recast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> as the father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a>, above all in Eugenio Aguirre's novel Gonzalo Guerrero of 1980.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gonzalo Guerrero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gonzalo Guerrero</b>, shipwrecked in 1511, married his daughter, adopted Maya customs and led its warriors against the Spanish; he died in Honduras around 1536.</blockquote><a href="/state/chetumal">Chetumal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palos de la Frontera, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pedro-sula">San Pedro Sula</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.23,-6.89&amp;year=1526" aria-label="Palos de la Frontera on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4377 823 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gonzalo Guerrero"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4827 1093h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4827 1093h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.23,-6.89&amp;year=1526">What was here in 1526? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
